next (unreleased) ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Language and compiler:
- behaviour change: && and || short circuit, the right side runs only when the left side
  does not decide the result (results are still 1 or 0)
- behaviour change: assigning to a literal, a library constant, a call result, the result
  of an operation or a member / element of a call result is WR_ERR_not_assignable (it
  used to change a copy or nothing)
- jumps and switch cases farther than 16 bits compile: a jump found too far is emitted in
  a long form (OX_RelativeJump32) in a further pass, far switch cases go through
  trampolines after the table; a switch past 32KB of code stored truncated positions
- extended opcodes: the prefix O_Extended is followed by a second opcode byte
  (WRExtendedOpcode), room for 256 more rarely executed opcodes now that the one byte
  space is full; OX_RelativeJump32 is the first
- a goto label defined twice in a function is WR_ERR_bad_goto_label
- the compiler prints nothing, the error message is only returned (errMsg); wrench c, ch,
  p and r print it
- string escapes documented on the website (\0NNN is a decimal character code)
- behaviour change: reading a member or element of a value that is no container gives 0
  and leaves the value alone (it turned the variable into an empty hash table or array);
  assignments still create containers (new opcodes O_IndexCreate, O_StackIndexHashCreate;
  images from older compilers keep the old behaviour, told apart by the new image flag
  WR_READS_KEEP_VALUES); a member of a literal is WR_ERR_member_of_literal
- the disassembler and the debugger's line table took LLValues, LGValues, GLValues and
  GGValues for one byte instead of three

Distribution:
- the single file build (wrench.h + wrench.cpp) is no longer kept in git: the old
  src/wrench.h|cpp and the copies in examples/arduino are gone. CI
  (.github/workflows/ci.yml) generates it, checks that it compiles with GCC and MSVC in
  several configurations, and attaches it to every release; "make dist" (or "wrench
  release src dist") writes it to dist/
- discrete_src/ is now src/: the sources live in src/, the generated single file build
  in dist/
- tests: the scripts are .w files; the list is tests/test_files.txt (entries relative to
  it, # comments, numbered from 1 without the old placeholder line); "wrench t
  [testdir] [number]" runs from any folder and the scripts run inside the test folder, so
  their scratch files no longer land in the repository root; a listed script that is
  missing counts as a failure, a missing list is an error instead of a crash; the
  internal host-seeded-global test read its file inside assert() and did nothing in
  release builds; unit_test1 (equality of all value kinds) and unit_test2 (string
  concatenation with globals) were never run and are part of the list now
- CI builds and tests Linux (default, compact, really compact, sanitizers) and Windows
  (Debug, Release, debugger / language server / extension tests) on every push
- the single file build compiles with WRENCH_WIN32_SERIAL and WRENCH_WIN32_TCP together
  (windows.h clashed with winsock2.h); the old Makefile is removed, GNUmakefile builds
  everything; examples/multi_context.cpp passed wr_getYieldInfo arguments in the wrong
  order

Debugger, rebuilt:
- new wire protocol (version 2): framed with sync bytes, length and CRC16 so UART links
  recover from noise, request/reply sequence numbers with retransmission, async events
  (stopped, output, ended, program loaded). Specification in docs/debugging.md
- WrenchDebugServer replaces WRDebugServerInterface: blocks inside the VM line hook while
  stopped (no forced yields), real frame walking for call stacks (works for code entered
  through wr_callFunction), pause, step in/over/out by frame depth, break on runtime
  errors with the stack intact, set variables, lazily expandable arrays/hashes/structs,
  program upload in chunks, reading the program/symbols/source back, host driven mode
  for firmware that calls into the script itself, no allocation while debugging
- WrenchDebugTransport byte interface with ready-made win32/linux TCP server/client and
  serial transports and an Arduino Stream transport, all declared in wrench.h so the
  single file build can use them; examples/debug_targets for Arduino, ESP32 WiFi,
  ESP-IDF and STM32 HAL
- WrenchDebugClient replaces WRDebugClientInterface (host side, C++17)
- wrench dbg: new console debugger (local, tcp:, serial: targets, attach mode, Ctrl+C pause)
- wrench dbgserver: run a debug target on a PC over TCP or serial
- wrench dap: Debug Adapter Protocol server (conditional/hit-count breakpoints, logpoints,
  watch/hover evaluation, set variable, debug console completion)
- wrench lsp: Language Server Protocol server (compiler diagnostics, hover, completion,
  signature help, definition, references, rename, outline, folding)
- tools/vscode-wrench: VS Code extension (grammar, LSP client, debugger integration)
- wrench.json describes the host application (its natives and the standard libraries it
  loads): the language server completes, documents and checks calls against it, the
  local debug target and dbgserver imitate that host (stand-ins for declared natives,
  everything else fails as in the application), launch option "project" / -project
- WrenchDebugServer::breakOnEntry( waitMs ): the next script code that runs halts on its
  first line, waiting for a debugger to attach first if none is (optionally bounded);
  wrench dbgserver -hold does the same on a PC
- WrenchDebugServer::setAllowUpload( false ) refuses programs sent by a debugger
  (new error WRD_ErrUploadDisabled, load capability not advertised); VS Code and
  wrench dbg explain it and point to attach; wrench dbgserver -noupload
- programs compiled with -debugsource debug without their .w files: wrench dbg -attach
  without a file reads program and source from the target (as VS Code attach without
  "program" did), and a .bin works as the program file for wrench dbg and wrench dap
  (launch uploads it unchanged, the embedded source is shown)
- runtime errors about missing functions name the function: the VM records the name hash
  (wr_getMissingFunctionHash()), Stopped/Ended events carry it, the debugger resolves it
- not-found handlers (wr_setOnCallbackNotFound / wr_setOnLibCallbackNotFound) can fail
  the call by setting w->err
- debug::print / debug::println send to the attached debugger and honour the newline
- for loops get a line marker on their condition, stepping now visits the loop header
- line numbers above 16383 are encoded with a prefix codeword instead of wrapping

Compiler fixes:
- peephole optimizer merged instructions across jump targets (ternary true arms,
  case labels), appendBytecode kept a stale opcode cache, optimizer wrote through a
  cleared cache in the fused compare-and-branch rules
- SwapTwoToTop stack bookkeeping did not match the VM (wrong operands in expressions)
- % and ^ precedence, compound assignments are right associative
- switch bodies ending in a loop or in "if ( x ) { break; }" fell back into the dispatch,
  negative case labels produced an empty table, case -1 was rejected
- an empty statement ";" popped a value it never pushed
- false "bad goto" after 32KB of code in a unit
- limits of the bytecode format are errors now instead of silent corruption:
  WR_ERR_too_many_locals, WR_ERR_too_many_functions, WR_ERR_code_too_large,
  WR_ERR_string_too_long, WR_ERR_duplicate_parameter; a hang with structs of more
  than 255 members is gone
- quoted ",", "{", "}" and ";" were treated as punctuation in some places
- back to back prefix operators (!!x, - -x, ~-x) were rejected
- enum constants replaced struct member names after '.' and in member declarations,
  redeclaring a constant with var is WR_ERR_constant_redefined
- character literals are unsigned ('\xFF' is 255), \a \b \f \v \xHH escapes,
  exponent notation (1e3, 2.5e-3) and 09.5 style decimals
- for( var v : <newline> arr ) failed to compile with debug code
- compile time was quadratic in the source length
- wr_compileEx() reports the line and column of an error

VM fixes:
- compound assignment to an array/hash element wrote the result into the right hand
  variable; comparing an int with a float turned the int variable into a float
- integer % by zero, INT_MIN / -1 and INT_MIN % -1 crashed; shift counts are taken mod 32;
  integer operators on floats left stale stack values
- every hash table leaked its first storage block; a hash table growing while a reference
  to one of its members was on the stack caused a use after free; removed keys came back
  with their old values; the GC marked uninitialized stack slots
- first local of a function was not zeroed
- contexts and their imports are garbage collected as one group (objects passed between
  them were freed), an imported function returning a local returned a dangling reference,
  wr_import into a yielded context or from a native callback overwrote live stack
- native callbacks may call wr_callFunction on the running context (frames were overwritten)
- stack overflow checks inside imports, time slices preempting an import call
- WRENCH_COMPACT: && and || with floats used a stale comparator
- array sizes are limited to WRENCH_MAX_ARRAY_ELEMENTS instead of overflowing allocations,
  hash tables stop at the largest table size instead of reading past the prime list
- wr_destroyContext leaked with a GC hint above 65535, WrenchScheduler leaked tasks that
  finished without yielding
- runtime errors go through one exit (WR_VM_FAIL) so the debugger can stop on them

- the GC recursed forever on a container that holds itself

Library / tools:
- msg:: mailboxes live in their own table (they could overwrite native callbacks)
- WRValue::asMallocString() reports the length of strings; WRValue::asString() treats
  maxLen as the buffer size (it wrote one byte past it)
- hash::exists / hash::remove return 1 or 0
- math::pow, atan2, fmod and ldexp take their arguments in C order (they were swapped)
- floats print rounded to at most 6 significant digits (like %g, 16.3818 instead of the
  truncated 16.38181), with nan / inf / exponent forms; INT_MIN prints correctly
- str::sprintf / format: %o and %b widths, sign before zero padding and counted in the
  width, %s of a non-string formats it, l/h/z modifiers skipped, width capped,
  WRENCH_FLOAT_SPRINTF path no longer overflows; str::printf of unterminated data
- std::rand over the full int range no longer divides by zero
- serializer: depth limit (WRENCH_MAX_SERIAL_DEPTH), malformed or truncated input is
  rejected cleanly, hash tables keep their counts, oversized containers are refused
  instead of truncated, allocation failures are handled
- array::clear zero-fills and bounds its count, list/stack/queue push store values (not
  references to the caller's variables), peek returns a copy, array::truncate checks args
- io::readFile, io::read, io::write (raw arrays), io::getline fixes; SPIFFS / LittleFS
  backend compile and memory fixes (untested here)
- tcp:: library: send used the wrong argument, receive turned errors into huge strings,
  win32 used file functions on sockets, peek, timeouts, failed host lookups, SIGPIPE
- WRstr: format reuse of va_list, 80 character boundary, self append, insert, find
- SimpleLL: removing during iteration skipped elements
- wrench c/ch/p: globals are named in the bytecode unless -noglobals, -debugsource implies
  -debug, compile errors exit with a failure status
- optional wrench_config.h next to wrench.h for project settings
- the test runner counts output mismatches as failures and runs every test a second time
  with debug code and an attached debug server; new tests 030/031 and a debugger protocol
  test suite (tools/test has DAP and LSP end to end tests)
- GNUmakefile builds the host tools (C++17) alongside the C++98 core

Second review (details and remaining items in docs/review.md):
- behaviour change: in strict mode a bare unknown name used as a value must name a script
  function (var t = cout + 1; is WR_ERR_var_not_seen_before_label instead of a hash)
- new errors WR_ERR_yield_in_native_call (script code a native callback called tried to
  yield), WR_ERR_too_many_arguments (a call with more than 255 arguments) and
  WR_ERR_nesting_too_deep (limit WRENCH_MAX_PARSE_DEPTH, default 256)
- compiler: continue inside foreach jumped outside the program, functions defined inside
  a block used freed memory, a nested { } ended the enclosing block, foreach past line
  16383 with -debug, ternary after a postfix operator, f().x and (s).x, variable keys in
  hash initializers, case (5):, -(-128) and -(-32768) folding, struct initializers past
  255 values, overflowing number literals are an error, binary and octal literals keep
  bit 31, escapes and comments no longer read past the source or recurse, -debug keeps
  stores the next statement overwrites, line markers per function, link() leaks
- VM: temporaries indexed by member (mk()["k"]) could be collected while referenced,
  adding two containers left a stale result, calling a library constant as a function,
  hashing or printing a container that holds itself, hash table counts and growth past
  the largest prime, float operands in %= |= &= ^= <<= >>=, signed overflow in *= ++ --,
  _exists on a temporary, wr_callFunction checks the stack, imports keep the caller's
  time slice, allocation failures no longer leave typed containers without data
- libraries: io:: on Windows no longer reaches the CRT invalid parameter handler,
  str::tprint and str::sprintf buffers, io::deleteFile, readFile and writeFile, serializer
  accounting, validation and growth, sys::importByteCode on bad input, sys::isFunction
  for constants, %f without float support, str::tol, debug::print without debug code,
  SPIFFS/LittleFS backend and FastLED fixes (not compiled for ESP32 here)
- debugger protocol: retransmissions are matched by type and sequence (a wrapped counter
  dropped commands), a stopped target re-announces the stop on Hello and Pause, Continue
  takes an optional flag to resume a step interrupted by a breakpoint, relaunch rebuilds
  the globals, value handles are reset whenever the program or the run changes
- WrenchDebugServer::setLinkTimeout( ms ): release a stopped target whose host stopped
  talking (the DAP adapter pings every second while stopped)
- tools: the DAP adapter survives malformed requests and huge bodies, false breakpoint
  conditions and logpoints no longer cancel a step, attach recompiles with the target's
  compiler flags, normalized breakpoint paths; wrench dbg no longer hangs on c or kill;
  non-ANSI paths on Windows, serial names and IPv6 hosts with ':', evaluator precedence,
  language server fixes (bare globals in functions, members per struct, unit, nesting,
  incremental indexing, UTF-16 positions, rename checks, UNC paths)
- the disassembler leaked its context; new tests 032 and 033, a compiler limits test

